From: Flax Advisors <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fbtp: shift the inlet fan curve +10C to match the real sensor

MB_INLET_REMOTE_TEMP is not an inlet sensor.  Measured on the bench 2026-08-17
with the room at 23.6 C (74 F):

    machine OFF, no airflow      INLET 40.6   INLET_REMOTE 39.2
                                 OUTLET 31.3  OUTLET_REMOTE 30.4
                                 AVA_F  29.6  AVA_R 29.0

The pair labelled "inlet" reads ABOVE the outlet, and with the fans unpowered it
RISES while every other sensor decays toward the room.  Air cannot enter warmer
than it leaves, and an intake sensor cannot warm up when the machine is off in a
still room -- these are board diodes soaking up local component heat, not
airflow.  The OEM firmware reads the same sensors at 38-42 C against a 31 C
outlet, so this is the hardware, not our configuration.

The offset is roughly +11 C with the fans at idle and +16 C with them stopped.
So this table, whose last step commands 70% at "40 C", reaches that step with the
room somewhere around 25-29 C.  An idle machine at ordinary room temperature was
sitting on its loudest step -- the fans were not misbehaving, they were being
told the intake was 40 C when the air was 25.

Shift the whole table +10 C, preserving its shape.  Conservative on purpose: the
measured offset is 11-16 C, so +10 leaves margin, and the compensation still
happens, just at a board temperature that corresponds to the ambient the curve
was drawn for.

This is safe because the inlet table protects nothing directly.  CPUs are held by
DTS_CPU*_Pid (setpoint 45) with a stepwise backstop at 73-91 C, and DIMMs by
their own backstop at 65-81 C; all are untouched.  The inlet curve is ambient
compensation -- raise the floor as intake warms -- and it is only that job which
was miscalibrated.

Left alone deliberately: the final step is still a cliff (35% -> 70% in one 2 C
move).  After the shift it fires at 50 C, far outside normal operation, so it is
no longer urgent; changing one variable at a time keeps this reversible.

OPEN: whether the offset is specific to this board or common to all TiogaPass.
Checking a Quanta server next -- if its sensor sits closer to ambient, this shift
must not go out fleet-wide as-is.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
 configurations/fbtp.json | 12 ++++++------
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/configurations/fbtp.json b/configurations/fbtp.json
index c522a0a..d75b1b0 100644
--- a/configurations/fbtp.json
+++ b/configurations/fbtp.json
@@ -575,17 +575,17 @@
       ],
       "PositiveHysteresis": 2.0,
       "Reading": [
-        20.0,
-        22.0,
-        24.0,
-        26.0,
-        28.0,
         30.0,
         32.0,
         34.0,
         36.0,
         38.0,
-        40.0
+        40.0,
+        42.0,
+        44.0,
+        46.0,
+        48.0,
+        50.0
       ],
       "Type": "Stepwise",
       "Zones": [
